<mujoco model="tabletop">
  <!--
    MuJoCo counterpart of tabletop.sdf: a static table plus 3 graspable cubes, matching that
    file's geometry/poses/colors exactly so downstream code (data collection, grasp targets)
    doesn't need per-backend numbers. See tabletop.sdf's own comments for the sourcing of
    those values (table position derived from tf2_echo against the robot's default pose,
    cube spacing chosen to avoid initial contact, friction mu=1.0 for grasp reliability).

    Robot include: MuJoCo composes a full simulation from ONE compiled MJCF, so the robot
    (arm+gripper+camera, generated from ur5e_2f85_camera_mujoco.urdf.xacro by
    make_mjcf_from_robot_description.py) has to be pulled in via a plain MJCF <include>,
    unlike Gazebo where the world runs standalone and the robot is spawned into it later at
    launch time. ROBOT_MJCF_PATH below is a placeholder - the launch file (not yet written,
    see the migration project memory's "not yet done" list) needs to either regenerate this
    scene file with the real path substituted in, or always output the robot's MJCF to a
    fixed, well-known path this <include> can reference directly. Until that's wired up, this
    file is validated standalone by hand-substituting a real path (see the migration memory
    for the exact validation command used).
  -->
  <include file="ROBOT_MJCF_PATH"/>

  <!-- Grasp-holding fix, not tuning: MuJoCo's default regularized friction model has NO true
       static-friction stick state - a pinched object drifts out of a grip at a roughly
       constant creep rate even when the load is well inside the Coulomb friction cone
       (confirmed against google-deepmind/mujoco#3328, which measured ~9mm/min creep at
       default solver settings for an analogous pinch-grasp case; matches exactly what we saw
       live - gripper closes and stalls correctly, but the cube slips back out during a lift,
       even via a manual rviz-driven MoveIt command with no scripted trajectory involved, so
       not a trajectory/planning artifact). Raising friction coefficients or contact
       stiffness (solref/solimp) only modestly helps this specific failure mode (per that
       issue's own measurements, ~3x at best) because the leak is in how MuJoCo's default
       solver iterates tangential contact forces, not in the friction cone itself being too
       small. The two effective fixes are both here:
         - noslip_iterations: a post-pass solver refinement specifically for this creep
           (~110x improvement in the referenced issue, 155 -> 1.4 micrometers/s creep).
         - impratio: biases the solver's impedance ratio between normal and tangential
           (frictional) constraints toward tangential, directly reducing slip (~10x
           improvement at impratio=10 in the same measurements).
       Global <option> settings (not per-geom), so they belong at the scene level, not
       per-object friction/solref tuning on the cubes below. -->
  <option noslip_iterations="10" impratio="10"/>

  <statistic center="-0.5 0 0.4" extent="1.2"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0.1 0.1 0.1"/>
    <global azimuth="120" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3"
      markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="5 5"/>
    <material name="table_wood" rgba="0.55 0.4 0.28 1"/>
    <material name="cube_red" rgba="0.8 0.1 0.1 1"/>
    <material name="cube_green" rgba="0.1 0.7 0.1 1"/>
    <material name="cube_blue" rgba="0.1 0.1 0.8 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 10" dir="-0.5 0.1 -0.9" directional="true" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8" specular="0.2 0.2 0.2"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="0 0 0.01" material="groundplane" contype="1" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!-- Table: 0.6x0.6m top, 0.3m tall (top surface at z=0.3; was 0.4m tall/z=0.4 - lowered
         10cm on request, which also directly helps a real reachability limit found during
         grasp testing: with the gripper's approach constrained close to vertical, cube_0's
         position leaves only ~1.5cm of vertical workspace above grasp height before the
         elbow nears full extension - see mujoco_grasp_test.py's own comments for the offline
         KDL IK + live OMPL evidence for this. Lowering the table moves the whole grasp height
         further from that fixed ceiling, buying more usable hover/lift clearance without
         changing the ceiling itself). Centered at x=-0.6, NOT tabletop.sdf's x=-0.5 (a
         deliberate MuJoCo-specific divergence from that file, which this comment used to
         claim exact parity with - no longer true for table/cube position, still true for
         size/mass/friction/color). Shifted 0.1m further from the robot base after confirming
         live + reproducing offline that the original -0.5 position let the robot's own
         upper_arm_link clip the table's near edge (contact at world x=-0.2003, i.e. exactly
         the table's boundary, dist=-0.0004 - a marginal but real collision) whenever the arm
         moved to a fully-vertical pose (e.g. MoveIt's SRDF-defined "home" state,
         shoulder_lift=-90deg with everything else 0) - not a software bug, the arm was
         genuinely about to swing through solid geometry and the simulation correctly stopped
         it via the self-collision-exclusion fix's ncon>0 path. -0.1m was chosen empirically
         (not calculated from first principles): confirmed to fully clear the contact (d.ncon
         shows zero table contacts at the settled home pose) while being a small fraction of
         the UR5e's ~850mm reach. Static: no <joint>, so it's welded to the world by default.
         MJCF box <size> is HALF-extents (0.6/2, 0.6/2, 0.3/2), unlike SDF's full-extent
         <size> - halved accordingly below. -->
    <body name="table" pos="-0.6 0 0.15">
      <geom type="box" size="0.3 0.3 0.15" material="table_wood"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Cubes: 4.5cm boxes (half-extent 0.0225), mass 0.065kg (light wood/plastic block,
         within the UR5e/2F-85's payload and gripper stroke - see the original cube_red
         model.sdf's own comment). No explicit <inertial>: MuJoCo's compiler auto-derives a
         uniform-density solid-box inertia tensor from geom shape + mass, which is exactly
         what the SDF's hand-computed I=(1/6)*m*a^2 formula also assumed - equivalent, less
         error-prone to let the compiler do it. friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001" (sliding,
         torsional, rolling) matches the SDF's mu=1.0 request for sliding friction (MuJoCo's
         own default happens to already be 1.0 there; set explicitly for clarity, not
         reliance on the default). <freejoint/> gives each cube unconstrained 6-DOF motion,
         and is what makes them addressable by name via mujoco_ros2_control's
         SetFreeJointState/ResetWorld services for episode resets (see the migration project
         memory - these are built-in services, no custom reset node needed, directly
         analogous to Gazebo's set_pose that tabletop.sdf's own comment described wanting).
         Initial poses (well-separated, ~0.1-0.2m apart) match tabletop.sdf's cube_0/1/2
         placements exactly. -->
    <body name="cube_0" pos="-0.7 -0.15 0.3225">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.0225 0.0225 0.0225" mass="0.065" material="cube_red" friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001"/>
    </body>
    <body name="cube_1" pos="-0.6 0.1 0.3225">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.0225 0.0225 0.0225" mass="0.065" material="cube_green" friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001"/>
    </body>
    <body name="cube_2" pos="-0.5 -0.1 0.3225">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.0225 0.0225 0.0225" mass="0.065" material="cube_blue" friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
